        <section id="what-is-source-code-control?" >
          
            <h2>What is Source code control?</h2>
              <p>Source code control (SCM) is a software <strong>version control system</strong> that helps developers to collaborate on code and keep track of changes.  This is useful and important in a team of developers working on the same software project.  If their individual work is not tracked, then this could create conflicts in the shared code and cause issues at a later stage of the project.</p>
<p>A source code management system (SCM) is a software tool that manages and co-ordinates a developer team’s code for a software project.  It provides file, configuration management (we cover this in more depth in Unit 5), and version control.  There are two main types of source control – distributed and centralised.</p>
<p>The below illustration is typical of source control branch flow such as Git or GitHub.</p>
<p><img src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s1_p3_image1.svg" alt=""></p>
<p>The <strong>Dev branch</strong> is the main 'working' branch that would be used by the team.  Each developer would have their own ‘Feature branch’ where they would work on a certain aspect of the code that was needed for the project. They would push to the ‘Dev branch’ once their 'feature' had been completed, reviewed, and tested.</p>
<p>If there were any bugs in the code then a independent ‘code fix’ branch would be created to keep this item separate. This fix would be merged into the 'Dev' and the 'Master' branches once completed, reviewed, and tested.</p>
<p>Once all the changes were done on the 'Dev branch' and had been reviewed, and tested, then it would be merged to the <strong>Master branch</strong>.</p>
<p>Once on the 'Master branch' it would have a final review and test.  It is at this stage that the stakeholders of the project would sign off that version, and then it would be deployed to the <strong>live site</strong>.  It is at this stage of the project that the 'version' of the software, application or website is recorded.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="source-control-management-solutions-(scm)"  class="t-bg-supporting3">
          
            <h2>Source Control Management solutions (SCM)</h2>
              <p>Source control tracks changes in source code. Source code management tools (SCM) track the changes to a source code <strong>repository</strong>, and maintains a running history of changes. This helps to <strong>resolve conflicts</strong> when updates are merged to the codebase from multiple developers working on one software project.</p>
<p>See below to learn more about SCM.</p>

              
              
  <div class="toggle-group">
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Common features
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The common features of most Source Control Management solutions (SCM) include:</p>
<ul>
<li>A <strong>repository</strong> which is where the source code is built, kept and managed</li>
<li><strong>Branching</strong> and merging which enables team members to work concurrently</li>
<li><strong>Traceability</strong> which means every change made to the code can be tracked, connected to project management tools and be tested for bugs.</li>
<li>The code is protected as the code is <strong>backed up</strong> to a remote location</li>
<li>Source control would include <strong>deployment</strong> to a remote repository and deployment sites can be integrated so the code can be reviewed on a local or dedicated site.</li>
</ul>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s1_p3_accordian1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          SCM strategy
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Having a source control management strategy can help minimise the cost and time of a project especially when working with a large developer team.  A <strong>branching strategy</strong> is something that a software developer team uses for a version control system to manage the codebase of a project.</p>
<p>Branching strategy for each developer working on a project team:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use branches and if working on a feature branch, check that you are on the right one</li>
<li>Commit often and include a commit message</li>
<li>Review changes before committing – either on a local site or a ‘Staging area’ on a deployment site such as DevOps or Netlify.</li>
<li>Check that you’re working on the latest version of the codebase before merging code to the main codebase (the ‘Dev’ branch).  This is especially important if working on a distributed source control as the branch will be local.</li>
<li>Make sure that all of the team has agreed upon a workflow and is working to a standardisation.</li>
</ul>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s1_p3_accordian2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Benefits
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The benefits of using SCM would include:</p>
<ul>
<li>It allows a team of developers to work on the same codebase</li>
<li>It keeps a complete version history and a regular backup of the code.</li>
<li>There is clearer communication and effective collaboration as every member of the team can see what each is working on.</li>
<li>It has workflow automation which means it manages the flow of tasks.  So for example a centralised workflow would use a central repository to serve as the single point-of-entry for all changes to a project.</li>
</ul>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s1_p3_accordian3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
</div>

            
              <p>The impact of not using a SCM could be vast especially if a developer team is large as not being able to return to a previous version of a codebase could be expensive and cause serious delays to a software project.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
