            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Leah:</strong> There are many source code control solutions available today, and it can become a little overwhelming when deciding which one is appropriate.</p>
<p>Some are open source and have a large supporting community of developers such as Git. and others are more business centred, focusing on larger teams and specific software such as Microsoft Team Foundation Server.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Having the right system is important, so there are key considerations that need to be made when choosing a source control, such as:</p>
<p>• which programming languages it supports.</p>
<p>• the popularity – this is a consideration as a popular solution such as Git has a large community which could be useful if support is needed, but also if a product is popular, it is more likely to be kept up to date.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> • is it fit for purpose – does it do what it needs to do for a given project?</p>
<p>• what tools and features it includes – does it have the necessary tools needed? For example, does it have a graphical user interface? Or does it include a bug tracking system?</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> • ease of use – how easy is it to learn and use? Is it intuitive? For example, GitHub is easier to use than its counterpart Git, due to its graphical user interface.</p>
<p>• hosting capabilities – is it a cloud-based solution? Is it open-source? How much storage is available for free and paid packages? This is quite a serious consideration as security and maintenance would also be a factor.</p>
<p>Some companies would choose to have their source code solution on their own servers, but there are also more modern Cloud solutions which will also offer built in security such as Azure DevOps.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> So, let’s have a look at some examples of the more well-known source code control solutions.</p>
<p>GitHub is one of the most popular online cloud-based hosting version control which is owned by Microsoft. It provides the distributed version control of Git plus ‘access control’, ‘bug tracing’, ‘task management’, and ‘continuous integration’. It provides a desktop application which has a graphical user interface, within which Git commands can be run without using the command line, so therefore simplifies the development workflow. As a free version it can be used for personal projects with smaller storage capacity, but there is the option to upgrade to a paid version which is more suitable for companies with larger storage requirements. It can manage multiple projects and has good documentation and support.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Git is an open-source distributed code control, that allows version control for a repository. It is good for intellectual software applications, easy to learn and setup, secure, and supports distributed versioning.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Bitbucket Server is good for teams already using ‘Atlassian’ products such as ‘Jira’. It suits team-based workspaces as well as being a versioning solution, and it includes project management, and collaboration. Whilst benefitting by having an intuitive GUI, and being easy to learn, integration with external systems such as a CMS, can be rather tricky.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Team Foundation Server known as Azure DevOps server is for teams that use ‘Microsoft tools’ and is designed for companies with its enterprise offerings. It integrates easily with existing Microsoft IDEs such as Visual Studio and project management tools. It can be expensive though and does not have as much flexibility when working with non-Microsoft tools.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Don’t forget to check out our website links to find out more information!</p>

